Subject: [S] Box-Cox transformation to stabilize the variance?
Message-ID: <3.0.3.32.19980306151518.006e416c@nceas.ucsb.edu>

Hi,

I've always though that using the Box-Cox method (using the likelihood-profile, 
e.g., as done in MASS) is a good way to find out about how to stabilize the 
variance (and thus implicitly get a grip on what the variance function looks 
like). 

Somebody recently queried me that if the Likelihhood profile assumes uses the 
Normal distribution, then there may be a problem in finding the transformation 
that acheives variance-stabilization - since, e.g.., with gamma distributed 
data the log-transform transform stabilizes the variance while the cube-root 
attains approximate normality.

So my question is: 
1) Is the box-cox likelihood-profile method (e.g., as in boxcox()) useless in 
finding a transformation to stabilize variances?
2) If so, what is a good method to find a power-transformation to stabilize the 
variance? 

