From zaslavsk at hcp.med.harvard.edu  Fri Mar 13 21:22:03 1998
From: zaslavsk at hcp.med.harvard.edu (Alan Zaslavsky)
Date: Fri, 13 Mar 1998 16:22:03 -0500
Subject: [S] virus warning hoaxes
Message-ID: <199803132122.QAA15375@nightingale.med.harvard.edu>

This sounds like a retread of a familiar hoax that goes under a number
of names, "Good times" being probably the first.

If you receive warnings about these, do not pass them on; they are
urban myths that do harm only by wasting people's time.  In effect the
warning is itself the virus!  Please check as described below before
acting on or passing on any such information, especially when received
from a non-expert source.

Information about the "Good Times" hoax in particular can be found at

    http://ciac.llnl.gov/ciac/notes/Notes09.shtml

You can also find an extensive discussion of these virus warnings and
the virus panic in general at the following Web site:
    http://www.kumite.com/myths/

Any person can go to the U.S. Department of Energy's Computer
Incident Advisory Capability web site:

     http://ciac.llnl.gov

to check on virus validity.  From that site, you can click and go to:

     http://ciac.llnl.gov/ciac/CIACHoaxes.html

to see the internet hoaxes.  Lastly, you can go to:

     http://ciac.llnl.gov/ciac/CIACVirusDatabase.html

to see a list of viruses and their validity/outcome.
